<article class="env-lemma" id="0904">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0904">Lemma <span data-tag="0904">5.23.7</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a spectral space. Let $x, y \in X$. Then either there exists a third point specializing to both $x$ and $y$, or there exist disjoint open neighbourhoods containing $x$ and $y$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\{ U_ i\} $ be the set of quasi-compact open neighbourhoods of $x$. A finite intersection of the $U_ i$ is another one. Let $\{ V_ j\} $ be the set of quasi-compact open neighbourhoods of $y$. A finite intersection of the $V_ j$ is another one. If $U_ i \cap V_ j$ is empty for some $i, j$ we are done. If not, then the intersection $U_ i \cap V_ j$ is nonempty for all $i$ and $j$. The sets $U_ i \cap V_ j$ are closed in the constructible topology on $X$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0901" data-tag="0901">5.23.2</a> we see that $\bigcap (U_ i \cap V_ j)$ is nonempty (Lemma <a href="/tag/005D" data-tag="005D">5.12.6</a>). Since $X$ is a sober space and $\{ U_ i\} $ is a fundamental system of open neighbourhoods of $x$, we see that $\bigcap U_ i$ is the set of generalizations of $x$. Similarly, $\bigcap V_ j$ is the set of generalizations of $y$. Thus any element of $\bigcap (U_ i \cap V_ j)$ specializes to both $x$ and $y$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>